                "id": "R4-fat-exec-bit-stale",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": "engine/skills/skillsfs/approvals.go:62-63",
                "severity": "WARNING",
                "claim": "The digest now depends on the owner execute bit, so on file systems that do not preserve it (FAT, CIFS, some Windows checkouts) every skill containing an executable file reads as APPROVAL_STALE and validate exits 1, with no fix-forward other than re-approving on a different machine. The candidate documents this limitation in the README, so it is an accepted trade-off by owner decision, but it is a real degradation mode for those environments. Not a regression on the supported path.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/skills/skillsfs/approvals.go:62-63",
                  "README.md:497-498"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "inferential",
                "causal_disposition": "introduced"
              },
              {
                "id": "R4-readregular-open-failure",
                "lens": "resilience",
                "location": "engine/skills/skillsfs/approvals.go:80-103",
                "severity": "SUGGESTION",
                "claim": "readRegular opens each file with OpenNoFollow and reads it fully via io.ReadAll. A file that vanishes between the directory walk and the open aborts the whole ReadSkillTree with an error. This is the same failure shape as the previous os.ReadFile, so it is not a regression, and the error surfaces to the caller rather than silently passing.",
                "proof_refs": [
                  "engine/skills/skillsfs/approvals.go:80-103"
                ],
                "evidence_class": "inferential",
                "causal_disposition": "pre-existing"
              }
            ],
            "evidence": [
              "Reviewed the whole candidate. The digest change is applied consistently: the domain (SkillDigest), the real adapter, the test double, the docs and the committed records all use the new line format.",
              "Failure handling is sound. A symlink or pipe swapped in after the listing is reported with its type and no bytes, so the domain refuses it as UNVERIFIABLE rather than blocking on a pipe or following the link. The execute bit and the bytes come from one descriptor, so there is no time-of-check/time-of-use gap between them.",
              "Names containing a backslash are now refused rather than silently producing a digest that the documented recipe cannot reproduce.",
              "Version 1 records are reported stale with an actionable message, so the migration fails closed with a clear remedy.",
              "The e2e test now requires validate to exit 0 on the real tree, which depends on every committed .approval.json matching the new digest. The candidate regenerates all of them with version 2, and nothing in the patch contradicts them. The digest values cannot be recomputed from this view."
            ]
          }
        }
